# PHASE 2: SMART CONTRACT REVERSE ENGINEERING (02-contracts/) ★ most important
Find the contract set from the frontend constants/bundles and from explorer tx history of real launch/buy/sell/graduate/claim txs. Then for EVERY contract:
- Identity: address, chain, deployer, deploy tx/block, proxy type and implementation, verified or not, license, compiler.
- Roles: factory, token implementation (clone/minimal-proxy vs full deploy), bonding-curve/market contract, router/quoter, fee splitter/treasury, creator-fee vault/claim, buyback executor, graduation/migrator, LP locker/burner, PONS token, staking/rewards, referral registry, oracle/price helpers, access-control owners/multisigs/timelocks.
- Interfaces: full ABI (recover from verified source or reconstruct from bytecode/selectors/events), function-by-function behavior, events, custom errors, storage layout where relevant.
- Math: derive and DOCUMENT the exact bonding-curve formula (constant product with virtual reserves? other?), initial reserves, supply split (curve vs LP vs creator vs fee), price/quote functions, rounding, fee rounding, slippage handling, max buy / anti-whale limits, graduation threshold and how it is checked.
- Flows (with sequence diagrams + a real traced tx for each): launch (incl. create-and-first-buy), buy, sell, creator-tax configuration, fee accrual/claim, graduation (curve → Uniswap pool: pool creation, liquidity seeding, LP lock/burn, price continuity), post-graduation fee handling, buyback trigger and execution, PONS token fee capture/distribution.
- Privileges & trust model: what can the owner/admin do (pause, change fees, upgrade, rescue funds)? Upgradeable or immutable? Timelock? This is the centralization map.
- Security posture: known issues, attack surface, how they handled "early attacks" (graduation front-running / pool pre-creation griefing, sniping, sandwiching, donation attacks, honeypot/malicious token metadata, fee-on-transfer, reentrancy, etc.). Reconstruct each incident's mechanism from public evidence.
- Gas profile per action. 
- Method: use Anvil/Foundry fork of the chain to simulate flows locally and check your inferred formulas against real on-chain results (write the fork tests in 02-contracts/verification/; these are allowed throwaway code). A formula is [ONCHAIN]-verified only if it reproduces real tx outputs exactly.
- Output: contract inventory table, per-contract spec sheets, architecture diagram, formula sheet with worked numeric examples, flow diagrams, trust/centralization map, incident analysis, and a "design lessons" list.

